{
  "A": "Own orientation and memory locality. Research O(min(m,n)^2 * max(m,n)) enumeration with contiguous scans, comparing the boundary-major prefix layout from 5e72c6518edd4d21 against incremental band accumulation from 44ba995a8f3f4d99. Their standalone measurements do not justify promotion, but hashing may have hidden locality costs. Evaluate layout changes with the winning scalar frequency workspace held constant. Allocate its capacity from the scan length, not the original row count. Keep prefix-layout bands independently schedulable; document that incremental accumulation requires private band storage and ordered extensions within each starting boundary. Retain only measured improvements.",
  "B": "Own the frequency-counting kernel; preserve 0ae23e9004bf4f65 as the incumbent. Prioritize scalar probing and reset overhead rather than the slower SIMD-hash variant. Compare contiguous frequency clearing against generation tags or touched-slot clearing individually; account for extra metadata accesses and safe generation rollover. Investigate a runtime k==0 path that uses one probe to read the old frequency and then increment it, preserving lookup-before-increment. Keep exact key comparisons, unsigned hash arithmetic, non-inserting failed queries, and sufficient capacity for scan_length+1 prefixes. Any alternative must handle negative keys and collisions correctly. Do not assume reduced clearing or fewer hash operations improves elapsed time.",
  "C": "Own four-core scheduling. Starting from d24154d242274e32, evaluate paired-start scheduling with a private B workspace and 64-bit subtotal per worker. Compare its dynamic tasks against static assignment of balanced paired starts and the flattened-band partitioning from c845a3c785cd40c3. Reassess serial/two-worker/four-worker thresholds because the faster counting kernel changes thread-overhead economics. Base dispatch on dimensions and available parallel work, not test identities. Respect configured limits and actual OpenMP team size; do not assume OpenMP is enabled or change compiler settings. Preserve a correct serial path and use only supported threading mechanisms. Keep synchronization outside band counting, and preserve A's incremental-band dependencies when applicable.",
  "G": "Integrate programs produced by the research trees, using scalar B candidate 0ae23e9004bf4f65 as the measured incumbent. First measure B plus C's paired-start scheduler, with one reusable table per worker. Then, within the existing budget, compare the alternative C scheduler and A layouts against their immediate parents; measure a three-way integration only after establishing useful component combinations. Merge components semantically rather than blindly applying overlapping src/main.cpp diffs. Recalculate table capacity and scheduling work after orientation changes, initialize prefix boundaries explicitly, and keep all mutable counting state private. Use the unchanged harness, oracle, compiler, target and budgets. Require correctness and measured whole-program improvement; retain B alone if integrations regress. Report component identities and measured integration results, never multiplied component speedups.",
  "analysis": "The target computation is Problem A: exact counting of nonempty contiguous submatrices, including negative entries. Baseline time is 0.250757 s. Scalar flat-table B reaches 0.073822 s, about 3.40x faster; paired-start C reaches 0.094293 s, while flattened C takes 0.103278 s. SIMD-hash B takes 0.098821 s, about 34% slower than scalar B. A's 0.249307/0.253154 s layouts remain near baseline. These results prioritize removing map overhead, then measuring interaction with four-core scheduling and locality; no integration measurements establish additive gains. Correctness requires seeding the zero prefix and querying before insertion. Under official bounds, absolute prefix sums are at most 998001000 and absolute query keys at most 1098001000, so signed 32-bit keys are safe. Frequencies are at most 1000, but the answer can reach 249500250000 and requires 64 bits. Existing measurements do not replace correctness reasoning for every valid input."
}